--- baseline/leadership_summary_text
+++ new/leadership_summary_text
@@ -0,0 +1 @@
+I lead IT the way I'd want it led if I were the business: close enough to the work to catch problems early, and far enough back to keep the roadmap tied to what the company actually needs next. At SK Life Science I joined pre-launch with two people and about $3M in spend, and built the function up through a first U.S. product launch to support $1B in commercial operations - so I know what it takes to go from nothing to something in a regulated environment, which is closer to where Upstream Bio sits today than the scale I finished at. I don't believe in strategy divorced from execution. I've written the technology roadmap and then sat with the vendor negotiating the contract that funds it, because in a growing biotech those are rarely two different jobs. Cybersecurity, data privacy, and audit readiness were never side projects for me - I owned ITGC design to SOX 404 standards, kept us inspection-ready, and never missed an SLA doing it. I also don't chase automation or AI for its own sake. Where it earned its place - reducing manual security triage, sharpening Medical Affairs engagement - I put it in, kept a human reviewing the output, and left the audit trail intact. That discipline matters more, not less, as a company moves toward its first commercial launch. People-wise, I lead through a small number of strong direct reports rather than a wide span of control, which keeps me available for the conversations that actually shift priorities: with the CFO, with R&D, with Quality, with the board when it's relevant. I'd bring the same posture to Upstream Bio - building a lean, fit-for-purpose IT foundation now, with the judgment to know what needs to scale ahead of Phase 2 readouts and eventual commercialization, and what can wait.